Title: MYTH OF THE STONES: THE SEVEN STONES
Credit: Draft 1 — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: ACT ONE of three. Proposed Canon v1.0. Every character, place and event here is a proposal; nothing is released, filmed or playable. Frequency motif: 9.1717 Hz.

FADE IN:

EXT. THE AURELITH — THE LAST DAY OF THE OLD WORLD

A white city on a cliff above a green sea. Terraces cascade down to harbours; bridges of pale stone cross canals that run uphill, because the Aurelians learned long ago that water goes where it is asked.

Above it, in a daytime sky, seven lights. Not stars. Seven steady points set in a ring, each a different colour, faint but unmistakable, and between them the sky is a shade deeper than elsewhere, as if the ring were a lens.

SUPER: "AT THE END OF THE FIRST AGE."

Children run along a ridge-top road, shrieking, carrying paper lanterns not yet lit. A woman laughs and calls them back. It is a festival: the day the seven lights align, which happens once in a lifetime, and everyone has taken the afternoon.

In a courtyard, a man of forty in a plain grey tunic sits on a fountain's edge with a small girl on his knee, folding a paper bird. This is MALACHAR. He is not yet anyone to fear. He has kind hands and an engineer's patient eyes.

GIRL
(six)
Papa, will it be loud?

MALACHAR
The alignment? No. It's the quietest thing in the world.

GIRL
Then why does Mama say you're nervous?

MALACHAR
(a small smile)
Because she knows me. Hold this.

He gives her the paper bird. She holds it up to the sky as if the seven lights might notice.

CUT TO:

INT. THE LOOM CHAMBER — CONTINUOUS

Beneath the city, a vault the size of a cathedral. In its centre hangs a mechanism of seven interlocked rings, each ring a different metal, each turning at a different pace, and where they cross the air seems to thicken. This is THE LOOM. It hums. The hum is low, even, and wrong in a way nobody has named yet: 9.1717 Hz.

Around the Loom, seven stones sit in seven cradles. Each is the size of a fist, translucent, faceted, and faintly alive: one the grey of winter water, one amber, one the deep green of old glass, and so on around the ring.

At the foot of the chamber, on a raised dais, stands the CONCLAVE: twelve robed rulers of the Aurelith, led by SPEAKER VERRAN, sixty, silver-haired, gracious, immovable. Behind them, seven figures in plain grey: the GUARDIANS, the engineers who built the Loom.

Among them: MALACHAR, the father we just met; a tall woman, ITHREN, fifty, steady; a spare, watchful elder, ERYNDOR; and four more: ORSOLYA, MAEVAR, TOLUN, SABETH.

SPEAKER VERRAN
Seven aspects, bound in seven stones. Creation, Memory, Time, Energy, Matter, Consciousness, Balance. For nine hundred years the Aurelith has tended them apart. Today, under the alignment, we bind them to one seat.

ITHREN
(low; she has said it before)
Speaker, the Loom was built to hold the stones apart.

SPEAKER VERRAN
The Loom was built to serve us, Guardian. Serve means answer. Why should the most powerful instrument in the world answer to nobody?

ITHREN
Because it answers to everybody, or it answers to a thief.

A ripple among the Conclave. Verran does not blink.

SPEAKER VERRAN
(gently)
The Guardians have kept the stones for nine hundred years. We thank you. Your service is complete.

He raises a hand. At the dais, a throne of black iron rises from the floor, its arms ending in seven empty sockets.

MALACHAR
(very quietly, to Ithren)
We can still stop this.

ITHREN
We built the Loom. Nobody can stop it but the stones themselves.

MALACHAR
Then we take them out.

ITHREN
(stricken)
You don't mean that.

MALACHAR
I mean exactly that. I have a daughter, Ithren.

On the dais, the Speaker seats himself on the black throne. The seven sockets glow. One by one, the seven stones begin to lift from their cradles toward him.

The Loom's hum climbs.

ERYNDOR
(a whisper, to herself)
Not like this.

Ithren steps forward and does what no one has done in nine hundred years. She reaches into the thickening air above the Loom and takes a stone out of its path.

The stone, grey, cold, lands in her palm. The Loom shudders. The Speaker, half-risen, cries out.

All around the vault, the six other Guardians move. They do not decide; they move, like a flock turning. Each seizes a stone from the air. The seven rings shriek.

The throne cracks from top to bottom.

CUT TO:

EXT. THE AURELITH — THE SAME INSTANT

In the sky, the seven lights go out, one after another, like a ring of candles pinched by a wet hand. The sky is, for an instant, perfectly black.

Then it tears.

A seam opens above the city, bright, jagged, the width of a river. Light that has no source pours out of it. Where it touches the sea, the sea stops, not frozen, simply arrested in the act of moving. Where it touches the terraces, old stone ages ten thousand years in a second and crumbles; a bridge becomes a handful of dust; a child's lantern flickers, ages, and is gone.

The woman who called the children back stands in the road with her hand out, and the road under her is a field of grass, and the grass is a desert, and she is a stain on the desert, and then nothing.

MALACHAR runs up the ridge road against a river of screaming people. Ahead of him the courtyard is a wasteland. The fountain is a stump. The little girl's paper bird lies on the dust, perfect, unharmed, the only thing in sight that is whole.

He falls to his knees.

MALACHAR
(a sound that is not a word)

CUT TO:

INT. THE LOOM CHAMBER — LATER

Smoke and silence. The Loom is dead, its seven rings frozen at odd angles. On the floor, the cracked throne; beside it, Verran's body, aged to bone in a robe.

The seven Guardians stand in a ring, each holding one stone. They are older now than they were an hour ago: grey in their hair, a hollowness in their faces. Nobody speaks.

At last Ithren breaks the silence.

ITHREN
We were supposed to keep them apart. We were supposed to be the ones who didn't need them.

MAEVAR
(young, shaking)
We saved what we could.

ITHREN
We saved a stone each. We saved nothing.

She lifts her stone, grey, quiet, and looks around the ring.

ITHREN (CONT'D)
They must never be together again. One hand will always want the whole. I will carry mine across the sea. You will carry yours. Seven roads, seven silences, no maps. If we hold them apart, nobody will ever hold the whole.

One by one, the Guardians nod. Orsolya weeps. Tolun, big, bearded, touches his stone to his forehead like a blessing. Sabeth turns away.

Malachar has not moved. He holds an amber stone in both hands. His eyes are dry.

MALACHAR
(quiet)
I won't.

ITHREN
Malachar —

MALACHAR
I will not carry it into a world that burned. I am going to put it back.

ITHREN
You cannot restore what is gone.

MALACHAR
The Loom made this. Perhaps the Loom can unmake it.
(a step toward her)
You want me to walk away from my daughter's grave.

ITHREN
(softly)
I want you to walk away from the throne.

He looks at her for a long moment. Then, without warning, he runs. Straight for the Loom, the amber stone raised to its dead rings.

Ithren's stone flares in her hand. A pulse of grey light, the colour of winter water, strikes him in the chest. He drops.

The ring of Guardians stands over him, aghast. Ithren, trembling, kneels beside him, her hand on his chest, the stone dull at her side.

ITHREN (CONT'D)
(whisper)
Forgive me.

Eryndor kneels at Malachar's other side. She looks old now, older than any of them. He lays two fingers on Malachar's brow.

ERYNDOR
He will sleep. I do not know for how long.

ITHREN
Then we hide him where nobody will think to look.

They lift him. The amber stone stays in his fist; nobody can open it. Behind them the ring of Guardians breaks and disperses, six paths, one body carried.

The camera pulls up through the dead Loom chamber's roof, through the ruined city, through a sky that is still bleeding light, and up, up into black.

SMASH CUT TO:

EXT. KHARESH BASIN — DAWN (PRESENT DAY)

A vast bowl of red rock under a hard sky. Wind. Tents, generators, grid-stakes: an archaeological camp. In the centre of the basin, a square pit forty feet across, lined with scaffolding, descending into rock.

SUPER: "FOUR THOUSAND YEARS LATER."

A drone buzzes over the pit, a camera on a tether. In the pit, a ramp, a door of black stone set into the rock, and, sprawled on the ramp with a laptop on her knee and a hard hat pushed back, DR. LYRA THORNE, thirty-eight, sunburnt, hair knotted with a pencil, a woman who sleeps in the field and does not complain about it. She is arguing with a screen.

LYRA
Tuan, tell me that's not an artefact.

TUAN PHAM (V.O.)
(over radio; tired, funny)
It's not an artefact. It's an anomaly in the data. Which I will remind you is a thing that happens when a pit has been dug for six weeks by people who want to find something.

LYRA
It's a pulse.

TUAN (V.O.)
It's a sixty-second repeating signal at nine point one seven one seven hertz. I looked it up. There's nothing in nature at nine point one seven one seven.

LYRA
So it's not natural.

TUAN (V.O.)
It's a refrigerator in your tent. Go look.

LYRA
(grinning)
I turned my refrigerator off at four.

A pause on the radio.

TUAN (V.O.)
(quietly)
Oh. Oh no.

On the ramp, behind Lyra, the black door has begun to hum.

EXT. KHARESH BASIN — THE PIT RIM — CONTINUOUS

HOLLIS REEVE, fifty-five, site director, weathered, stands at the rim with a clipboard and a cigarette he is not supposed to have. Beside him, in a pressed field jacket that has never seen dirt, TORIN DRAVEN, forty-two, calm, courtly, with a soldier's stillness and a scholar's eyes. He carries no weapon; he looks as if he does not need one.

HOLLIS
Mr Draven. The funders sent you to watch.

TORIN
The funders sent me to keep everyone alive.

HOLLIS
And what do you think of a refrigerator that sings?

TORIN
(gazing at the door)
I think it is the first honest thing this basin has said.

He says it lightly. He is not joking.

INT. THE BURIED TEMPLE — DESCENT

Lyra goes down alone, headlamp swinging, past the door that has opened without any hinge she can find. Beyond it, a long corridor cut from the living rock. The hum is louder here. Her teeth ache with it.

The walls are carved. Not with pictures, but with rings: concentric circles, nine of them, nested like a target. Seven of the nine are filled with fine, dense glyphs. Two are smooth and blank.

LYRA
(to herself, recording on a pocket device)
Nine rings. Seven inscribed. Two vacant. Not erased: never filled. That's either an unfinished text or a deliberate omission.

She runs a gloved finger along the lowest ring. The glyphs are not a language she knows. They are, she realises, a lattice: a diagram of nodes joined by lines.

LYRA (CONT'D)
(a breath)
It's a network.

The corridor ends in a round chamber under a shallow dome. In the centre, on a pedestal of the same black stone, rests a cradle, and in the cradle, a stone.

It is the size of a fist, grey, faintly translucent, like water frozen in the act of thought. Etched into the pedestal below it, in clean modern-looking lines that have no business being four thousand years old, are five numerals:

91717.

Lyra stops. Her headlamp trembles.

LYRA (CONT'D)
(whisper)
Hello.

Her better judgement says glove up. She takes the glove off anyway, because she is a scientist and a person and cannot be both at once.

She touches the stone.

The chamber goes white.

CUT TO:

INT. MUSEUM CONSERVATION LAB, PORT VESSA — NIGHT

A basement workshop under a coastal museum: benches, loupes, trays of tiny bronze fittings under a lamp. At the far bench, in a canvas apron, a young man of twenty-four is cleaning a Roman coin with a dental pick. This is AUREN VALE: thin, quiet, shadowed under the eyes, a man who looks as though he has been listening to something for a long time.

A colleague, NELL, calls from the door.

NELL
Vale, you're still here. It's midnight.

AUREN
I'm almost done with the hoard.

NELL
You said that at nine.

AUREN
(smiling faintly, without looking up)
I'm always almost done.

Nell goes. The basement is quiet. Auren puts down the pick, rubs his eyes, and for a second his hands are not steady. He closes them into fists. Breathes.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(under his breath, a habit)
Not now. Not now.

On the bench, the coins begin to hum.

He stares. All thirty-one coins in the tray vibrate, each at a slightly different pitch, then converge: one note. The lamp swings on its arm. A glass of water on the shelf trembles into rings.

The world goes white.

INT. THE LOOM CHAMBER — VISION

Auren stands in the dead Loom chamber of the Aurelith, watching. He cannot move. Before him a tall woman in grey, ITHREN, kneels, a stone dull at her side, her hand on a man's chest.

ITHREN
(whisper)
Forgive me.

She looks up. Looks straight at Auren as if she can see him.

ITHREN (CONT'D)
(a different voice, close, personal)
Hold the other end.

He gasps.

INT. MUSEUM CONSERVATION LAB — CONTINUOUS

Auren is on the floor of the lab, on his back, in a spill of coins. The glass has fallen. His nose is bleeding. The hum has stopped.

He sits up. Wipes his nose. Looks at his hand. Looks at the coins. There is a very long moment in which he does not appear to breathe.

AUREN
(softly)
Mum.

INT. AUREN'S APARTMENT, PORT VESSA — LATER

A small flat above a fishmonger's: bare walls, a narrow bed, a kettle. On the shelf, a single framed photograph of a woman in a headscarf laughing at the camera, a young boy on her hip. The woman is ISOLDE VALE. She has been gone fifteen years.

Auren sits at the table with a battered tin box open before him: a lock of hair, a child's drawing, a folded paper. He unfolds the paper. It is a page from a field notebook, in his mother's slanted hand. In the margin, a sketch: nine concentric rings, seven filled, two empty. Under it, five numerals. And a word: Kharesh.

AUREN
(reading)
"If you ever hear it, don't follow it. Let it pass."

(He turns the paper over. On the back, in smaller letters:)

AUREN (CONT'D)
"...but if you can't, go to the ones who dig."

He looks at the drawing a long time. Then at the photograph. Then he stands, takes a coat, and puts the paper in his pocket.

EXT. KHARESH BASIN — DAY

The camp is in chaos. Lyra sits on the tailgate of a jeep, wrapped in a blanket, a medic shining a light in her eyes. She is dazed but alive. In her lap, wrapped in a camp towel, is the grey stone.

HOLLIS
(crouching beside her)
The whole damn pit lit up. We thought you were dead.

LYRA
(wonder)
It was warm.

HOLLIS
What was?

LYRA
Everything.

TORIN
(approaching, quiet)
Dr Thorne. We should discuss moving the find.

LYRA
It's not a find, it's...
(she looks at the towel)
It's a person's thing. Somebody made it.

TORIN
Yes. And somebody will come for it.

She looks up at him sharply. His face is composed. He is, in all outward ways, the very picture of a man who is only worried.

TUAN
(running up with a laptop)
Lyra. Lyra, the signal's moved.

LYRA
What do you mean, moved?

TUAN
It was coming from the pit. Now it's coming from your lap.

A pause. They both look at the stone.

A jeep skids to a halt at the edge of the camp. Out of it climbs Auren Vale in a city coat, pale, clutching a paper. He looks at Lyra. At the stone. At the pit.

AUREN
(hoarse)
I'm looking for the people who dig.

LYRA
Who are you?

AUREN
(helplessly)
I think I'm what's left.

EXT. KHARESH BASIN — THE RIDGE — SAME MOMENT

Three black vehicles crest the ridge above the camp, low-slung, unmarked. They stop in a line. Doors open. Men and women in dust-coloured field gear step out, calm, fast, armed with something quieter than rifles.

At their head, VESPER LUND, thirty-five, sharp, bareheaded, and beside her a heavy man, CORVIN HALE, forty-eight, scarred about the neck. Vesper lowers a pair of binoculars.

VESPER
(into a radio)
Confirmed. It's out of the ground.

MALACHAR (V.O.)
(radio, warm, unhurried, a voice you would trust with your life)
Bring it to me. Hurt nobody you do not have to.

VESPER
And the boy?

MALACHAR (V.O.)
(a beat)
What boy?

VESPER
A young man arrived in a hired jeep. He matches the photograph.

A long silence on the line.

MALACHAR (V.O.)
Bring him too. Gently.

EXT. KHARESH BASIN — CAMP — CONTINUOUS

The first shot is not a shot. It is a hiss: a dart, a small thing, and Hollis Reeve folds sideways without a sound. A second, a third; two diggers drop on the sand.

LYRA
(on her feet)
They're not firing, they're —

TORIN
Down.

He is already moving. He takes Lyra by the collar and hauls her behind the jeep as a dart strikes the metal where her head was. He is astonishingly quick. He is also, as he moves, looking everywhere except at the attackers, a man deliberately not seeing something he cannot afford to see.

AUREN
(running up; stunned)
Who are they?

TORIN
(a lie that arrives too fast)
Looters.

He throws Auren into the jeep's rear seat, shoves Lyra in beside him, and slams the door.

TUAN
(shouting from the next tent)
Lyra! The pit! There's a back tunnel! It runs under the ridge!

LYRA
(to Torin)
The stone —

TORIN
Have you got it?

LYRA
(holding up the towel)
Yes!

TORIN
(a small, controlled breath)
Then go.

EXT. THE PIT — RAMP — CONTINUOUS

Four people sprint down the ramp toward the black door, Torin last, taking the rear, firing nothing, covering everything. Vesper's team spills over the rim behind them, darting, shouting orders.

Corvin Hale roars and lunges to the rim, hurling a flare into the pit. It strikes the scaffolding and explodes in white light.

INT. THE BURIED TEMPLE — CORRIDOR — CONTINUOUS

The corridor shakes. Dust rains. They run: Lyra clutching the stone, Auren behind her, Tuan in the rear with a laptop under his arm and a camera in his teeth. The nine-ringed walls flash past.

LYRA
(over her shoulder)
Where does it go?

TUAN
(muffled; the camera's in his mouth)
Two kilometres! The ridge! There's a ventilation shaft!

TORIN
(arriving at the back of the pack)
Not a vent. A route. They built this to be left by.

Behind them, the corridor lights up with torch-beams. Shouts echo. A dart hits Tuan's laptop, which explodes in a small shower of plastic.

TUAN
(shrieking)
My data!

AUREN
(grabbing him)
Run!

They hit the round chamber. The pedestal is empty. The cradle is bare. Auren, drawn by something he cannot name, stops in the centre and puts his hand flat on the pedestal.

LYRA
Auren! Move!

He does not move. He hears the hum. It is no longer a hum; it is a voice, a woman's, many years old, calm.

ITHREN (V.O.)
(whisper)
Hold the other end.

Auren's hand flares white against the black stone. In Lyra's arms, the grey stone blazes.

CUT TO:

INT. THE BURIED TEMPLE — THE ROUND CHAMBER — CONTINUOUS (TIME-SLIP)

The world slows.

Not slows: stops. A dart, in mid-flight, hangs a hand's breadth from Lyra's face, its fletching trembling in the air like a hummingbird frozen mid-beat. Tuan, mouth open in a shout, is a statue. Torin is a statue. The dust is a still constellation.

Auren and Lyra alone move. They look at each other.

LYRA
(whisper)
It's doing this.

AUREN
No. I am.

LYRA
Can you stop it?

AUREN
(helpless)
I don't know how I started it.

A tiny hairline crack runs across the grey stone in Lyra's hands. The hum shivers. Time lurches, one frame, two. The dart jolts a quarter inch nearer.

LYRA
(urgent)
Auren. Whatever you're doing, you're running out.

Auren closes his eyes and does what a child does when the world is too large: he thinks of his mother's face. The world grinds back into motion.

The dart whistles past Lyra's ear and buries itself in the pedestal.

TUAN
(finishing the shout)
— DATA!

TORIN
(very quietly, staring at Auren)
Dear God.

LYRA
(grabbing Auren's sleeve)
Go go go.

They run for the far side of the chamber, where a narrow slot of a doorway breathes cool air. Behind them, Vesper's team bursts in, torches sweeping the empty chamber.

EXT. THE RIDGE — VENT SHAFT — DAY

A circular shaft cut into rock opens onto a hillside, hidden among thornbushes. One by one, four dusty figures crawl out: Lyra, Auren, Tuan, Torin. They lie in the scrub, gasping.

Below them, distantly, the camp burns.

LYRA
(flat)
Hollis.

TORIN
He was alive. They used sedation. They want us alive.

LYRA
(rounding on him)
How do you know that?

Torin meets her eyes. For a moment the courtesy cracks.

TORIN
Because that's what I would do.

He looks away. She keeps looking at him, uneasy, but there is no time.

TUAN
(rolling over; the camera is still in his hand)
For the record, I am now, officially, a believer.

AUREN
(sitting up, shaking, holding his own wrist like something that might leave)
I stopped time.

LYRA
You paused it. For two seconds. In a tomb.

AUREN
How do I do that?

LYRA
I don't know. Who taught you?

AUREN
Nobody.

LYRA
(slowly)
Then somebody built you to.

INT. A BOOKSHOP UNDER A RAILWAY ARCH, PORT VESSA — NIGHT

Shelves to the ceiling. A single green-shaded lamp. The air smells of tea and paper and a century of lost afternoons. A bell on the door rings as it shuts, and four exhausted travellers stand dripping in the aisle.

Behind a counter, a woman looks up. SERAPHINE is somewhere between fifty and seventy, grey-streaked hair in a loose knot, large spectacles, a man's cardigan, a pencil behind her ear. She looks at them over the rims of her glasses with the unhurried gaze of a person who has been expecting them for longer than they have been alive.

SERAPHINE
You're late.

LYRA
Excuse me?

SERAPHINE
By about fifteen years, if I'm counting the boy. Shut the door properly, dear, the draught is wicked.

AUREN
(stepping forward)
Do I know you?

SERAPHINE
(a tiny flicker, quickly masked)
You have your mother's chin.

Auren goes very still.

AUREN
You knew her?

SERAPHINE
I knew her mother. And hers. Sit down.

TORIN
(from the doorway, hand near his coat)
Who are you?

SERAPHINE
(without turning)
Someone with a kettle, Mr Draven. And you needn't pretend. I know what you are.

A silence in which Torin's hand does not move. Lyra looks between them.

LYRA
What is he?

SERAPHINE
A former Warden. Fallen out of the old order twenty years ago. Hired by the funders. Who are not funders, I'm afraid, but a shell of a shell of a man who has been looking for that stone for three hundred years.

LYRA
(slowly)
Three hundred.

SERAPHINE
Roughly.

TORIN
(a long beat; a controlled breath)
She is correct that I was a Warden.

SERAPHINE
And correct about the funders?

TORIN
(carefully)
I did not know who they were.

SERAPHINE
(gently)
Of course you didn't.

She puts the kettle on a hob in the back, takes down five cups, and does not turn around.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
Show me the stone.

Lyra hesitates. Then she sets the towel on the counter and unwraps it. The grey stone sits in the lamplight, faintly translucent, with a hairline crack across one face.

Seraphine regards it for a long time. Her hands, resting on the counter, tremble very slightly.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
(quiet)
Memory.

LYRA
Sorry?

SERAPHINE
The first of seven. It holds what has happened and will not let it be forgotten. That is why the boy dreams it.

AUREN
You know about the dreams.

SERAPHINE
Every child of the Guardians has them. Yours were worse than most. You are a very direct descendant.

AUREN
Of what?

SERAPHINE
(pouring tea)
Of the woman who took that stone out of the sky.

She sets a cup in front of him. He does not touch it.

AUREN
(hoarse)
Ithren.

SERAPHINE
(a very small, stunned pause)
Yes. How did you...?

AUREN
She told me. In the vision. She said hold the other end.

Seraphine puts the kettle down very carefully. Her face does something that is not quite a smile and not quite grief.

SERAPHINE
Then she is still speaking. Good. That's more than the rest of us managed.

She comes around the counter, spreads a large, soft, brittle map across a reading table, and weights its corners with teacups. It is not a map of any country. It is a web of seven nodes and a spidery tracery of lines.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
Seven stones. Seven aspects of a single mechanism: Creation. Memory. Time. Energy. Matter. Consciousness. Balance. They are not weapons. They are the working parts of a thing that was built to keep the world from being owned.

LYRA
(slowly, leaning over)
A network.

SERAPHINE
A distributed key. No one stone does anything alone. No one hand may hold more than one, and no hand should hold even that for long.

LYRA
Why?

SERAPHINE
Because the last time a hand held all seven, the sky tore.

Silence. Outside, the rain begins.

AUREN
(very quietly)
The Sundering.

SERAPHINE
You say it as if you have seen it.

AUREN
I have.

SERAPHINE
(looking at him with a long, long tenderness)
Yes. I rather think you have.

She lifts her tea, drinks, sets it down. Her hand is steady now.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
The stones were scattered after. Nobody was meant to find more than one. Somebody has been finding them for three hundred years.

TORIN
(very low)
Malachar.

Seraphine glances at him sharply.

SERAPHINE
You know the name.

TORIN
(a beat too long)
It's in the old Warden texts.

SERAPHINE
(softly)
So it is.

She says nothing more. She is watching him in the way a person watches a coin that has not yet landed.

INT. A SANCTUM BENEATH A RUINED ABBEY — NIGHT

Stone vault. Candles. A long table. Seven niches in the wall, each shaped to take a stone. Two of them already hold one: an amber stone, warm as honey, and a stone the dull red of old brick.

Before the niches stands MALACHAR. In the present he looks forty, as he did on the morning of the Sundering, though his eyes are four thousand years old. He wears a plain grey coat. His hands are the same patient, engineer's hands.

Vesper Lund stands before him, hat in hand.

VESPER
We lost them in the tunnels. The boy stopped time.

MALACHAR
(a small, wondering stillness)
Did he.

VESPER
For two seconds. Corvin saw it. It threw the whole team.

Malachar's face does something complicated. Hope. Fear. Something like homesickness.

MALACHAR
Ithren's line.

VESPER
We think so. The Warden confirmed it.

Malachar turns to the niches. Touches the amber stone, Time, with one knuckle. It warms under his hand.

MALACHAR
(very gently)
You are not to harm the boy.

VESPER
(a flicker)
Sir, he is carrying the stone.

MALACHAR
He is carrying something older than the stone.

He takes a small thing from his breast pocket, a paper bird, crumpled with age, impossibly preserved, and sets it on the table.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
They think I want to destroy the world.

VESPER
(carefully)
Some do.

MALACHAR
I do not want to destroy it.

He looks at the two stones in their niches. Then at the five empty ones.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
I want it back.

CUT TO:

INT. A BOOKSHOP UNDER A RAILWAY ARCH — LATE NIGHT

The shop is dark but for the green lamp. Lyra sleeps on a sofa under a coat. Tuan sleeps at the reading table with his head on his arms. Torin stands at the window, looking out at the rain.

Auren sits across from Seraphine at the counter. Between them, the cracked grey stone. Neither speaks for a long time.

AUREN
You haven't told me everything.

SERAPHINE
No.

AUREN
Will you?

SERAPHINE
(gently)
Not tonight.

AUREN
Why not?

SERAPHINE
Because tonight you need to sleep, and tomorrow you will need to choose, and I would rather you chose with a clear head.

AUREN
Choose what?

SERAPHINE
Whether to go and find the other six.

AUREN
(a short laugh)
I stopped time for two seconds and bled on a pedestal. I'm not a hero. I clean coins.

SERAPHINE
(drily)
Ithren cleaned coins too.

AUREN
(looking up)
Did she?

SERAPHINE
She was a metalworker before she was a Guardian. Not a very good one. She said she liked the small things best.

Auren looks at the stone. At the crack running across it.

AUREN
If I don't go?

SERAPHINE
(quiet)
Then he will. He will find all seven. And he'll be so very kind about it.

AUREN
(very still)
Kind.

SERAPHINE
That is the part that will frighten you most, in the end.

She rises, switches off the lamp, and the shop goes dark. In the dark, softly, the grey stone glows. The crack along its face has begun, very slowly, to close.

END OF ACT ONE
